  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a low autocorrelation-based initialization combined with a hybrid optimization framework that incorporates both gradient-based refinement and exploratory perturbations. This addresses the non-convexity of the problem by leveraging structured initial guesses (density profiles derived from low autocorrelation sequences) and iterative updates that balance local improvements with global exploration. The previous methods' limitation of suboptimal local convergence is mitigated by using a dual-phase optimization: first, a gradient-based refiner operating on a smooth surrogate, followed by post-optimization perturbations that explicitly explore the constraints. The initialization uses evenly spaced binary events with adjusted frequencies to create a baseline that implicitly minimizes overlap across common lags.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: \n- h_flat (n_points-length vector) with 0 \u2264 h_flat[i] \u2264 1\n- Target sum: target_sum = n_points / 2\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x) * (1 - h(x + k)) dx, approximated via np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx\n\nSurrogate: C\u2085_approx = log(\u03a3 exp(\u03b1 * overlap_k)) / \u03b1 for \u03b1 = 5000\n\nConstraints:\n- \u2211h_flat = target_sum\n- 0 \u2264 h_flat[i] \u2264 1\n\nOptimization:\n- SLSQP with bounds [0, 1] and equality constraint \u2211h_flat = target_sum\n- Post-optimization validation using exact evaluator\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent Validation:\n- If initial_h_values is provided, use it\n- Else: Generate binary pattern by placing 1's at even intervals: h_flat[i] = 1 where i \u2261 0 mod (n_points // 2), then scale to \u2211h_flat = target_sum\n\nInitialization:\n- For even n_points, set h_flat[i] = 1 for half the positions spaced at intervals of 2/(n_points/2)\n- For odd n_points, use a slightly modified spacing (e.g., 2/(n_points - 1) for the first n_points-1 points)\n\nOptimization Loop:\n- Stage 1 (Surrogate-based):\n   - Run SLSQP with \u03b1=5000 (log-sum-exp surrogate)\n   - Use maxiter=200 and tolerance=1e-8\n   - Post-iteration: Apply structured perturbations (swap 0/1 at positions with high overlap)\n- Stage 2 (Exact-based):\n   - Switch to exact objective evaluation\n   - Run L-BFGS-B with maxiter=50\n   - Apply Gaussian noise (\u03c3=0.02) every 10 iterations, then clamp and rescale\n\nCandidate Acceptance:\n- Track best solution (lowest C\u2085)\n- Validate \u2211h_flat \u2248 target_sum (tolerance 1e-6)\n- Ensure all h_flat[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n\nTermination:\n- Stop when time budget is exhausted or no improvement in 10 iterations\n- Return best solution and its C\u2085 bound\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points=200\n- Stage 1: SLSQP with \u03b1=5000, 200 iterations, structured perturbations\n- Stage 2: L-BFGS-B with 50 iterations, Gaussian noise every 10 iterations\n- Perturbations: swap 0/1 at positions with high overlap (determined via max overlap index)\n\nVariations:\n- Binary initialization with denser/further spacing (m = n_points//3 to m = n_points//2)\n- Surrogate \u03b1 values: 2500, 5000, 10000\n- Solver combinations: SLSQP + L-BFGS-B\n- Noise patterns: uniform, Gaussian, Cauchy\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Stage 1: 200 iterations with SLSQP (each iteration ~1s) = ~200s\n- Stage 2: 50 iterations with L-BFGS-B (each iteration ~1s) = ~50s\n- Perturbations: 100s\n- Final refinement: 150s\n\nRobustness:\n- For nan values: clamp to [0,1], rescale\n- For constraint violation: project to feasible region by adjusting values\n- Fallback: use initial_h_values if all optimizations fail\n- Early exit: if no improvement for 10 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- \u2211h_flat \u2248 target_sum (tolerance 1e-6)\n- All h_flat[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- C\u2085 bound is finite and matches exact evaluator\n\nFinal validation:\n- Re-evaluate candidate with exact objective\n- Compare with current best\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points=200",
